this massive metropolis in the center of  China is at the heart of a virus  outbreak that has gone global  the first death from the new corona  virus outside of China cruise ship the  diamond princess remains quarantined in  Yokohama more than 71,000 people have  been infected around the world  in February Chinese officials and state  media touted a drop in the number of  daily new confirmed cases  but only hours later local authorities  released data showing a dramatic jump  partially due to a shift in the way new  cases were diagnosed and confirmed while  the authorities said the goal behind the  new criteria was to let more patients  receive better treatment this raised  concerns that the scale of the outbreak  has been larger than Chinese data had  earlier suggested we thought there was  better transparency coming out of China  but it doesn't appear to be in response  to the criticism Beijing said it has  been active and open toward cooperation  with international experts to  independently assess the scale of the  disease groups of scientists around the  world have been using mathematical  modeling and their estimate so far  exceed the officially reported numbers  the new virus was first detected in  December and by mid-january  the number of cases was just over 40 but  epidemiologists across the world thought  that something wasn't right when three  cases were confirmed outside of China  cases when there seemed to be relatively  small numbers Krystal Donnelly and her  colleagues at the Imperial College  London looked at the number of cases  outside China which they call exported  to calculate the scale of the outbreak  what kinds of big cities million people  in the metropolitan area and it has an  international airport and it's  interconnected so so their flights and  people traveling to lots of places they  used similar mathematical modelling  developed during the h1n1 influenza  outbreak in Mexico in 2009 you can look  at the number of cases that have been  exported and then there is a factor of  which you can use to then estimate the  number of cases that there were in the  source region  before the virus shut down the city the  International Airport operated flights  to dozens of major cities worldwide with  more than 3,000 passengers traveling  through the airport every day the  researchers also assumed that 10 days  likely passed between a person getting  infected and the disease being detected  but you can get an idea by looking at  the number of flights you know what's  the population size how many departures  international departures do you have on  a giving day the imperial college  researchers estimated that over 1700  people could have been infected in Wuhan  by mid January indicating a massive gap  between China's 41 confirmed cases  reported at that time and their  estimates other researchers did similar  calculations based on the exported  overseas cases and with several other  variables and came to similar results in  later reports scientists factored in new  data and their numbers were still much  bigger than Beijing's official tally of  confirmed cases these findings give a  rough picture and the margin of error  remains high in part because of other  factors like the fact that human to  human transmission was confirmed  overseas many people in China have been  critical of how Beijing has dealt with  the crisis and have been skeptical about  the official data Donnelly says that the  Imperial colleges analysis is not aimed  at criticising Beijing's efforts but  trying to look at what was going on and  what might be expected from the virus  she says that there are a myriad of  reasons why China's figures might not  reflect reality it's difficult for any  country the new disease starts in your  country there's a lot to be identified  to begin with so when it first starts  really don't know what is potential is  what makes it even harder to diagnose  the corona virus is that people infected  may show very mild symptoms like fever  and coughing there are also common in  other types of infections in an effort  to battle the virus Beijing has spent  about eight hundred million dollars  deployed thousands of medical staff and  built two field hospitals in days China  is doing many good things that slowing  the fight  and this has to be recognized well the  w-h-o repeatedly praised China other  public health experts have been more  critical it took the agency nearly two  weeks to get a go-ahead from Beijing to  send a group of experts to China to  conduct joint research with Chinese  scientists epidemiologists say they are  still learning about the new virus and  hope their research will help officials  in China to put effective measures in  place to tackle the outbreak  [Music]
 

